WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.860
From creature features to gritty war&nbsp;
stories, these movie series have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:08.460
cranking out low-budget sequels&nbsp;
for a surprisingly long time.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:13.560
A divisive crucible of infernal frights,&nbsp;
Clive Barker's directorial debut "Hellraiser"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:18.000
is regarded as either a miracle of&nbsp;
movie horror or an overly bloody mess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.100
depending on who you ask. Is it possible&nbsp;
for both to be true? Regardless of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:28.020
film's quality — which is mostly fine —&nbsp;
"Hellraiser" is undeniably visually striking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:32.040
debuting Pinhead and the Cenobites in&nbsp;
this gnarly vision of industrial hell.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.960
But if there's any debate about the quality&nbsp;
of the original, there's little to none about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.340
the following nine sequels. "Hellbound:&nbsp;
Hellraiser II" is a pretty good time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:45.480
but after that? They're awful in&nbsp;
all sorts of interesting ways.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.534
"We have such sights to show you."

00:00:49.534 --> 00:00:54.300
Want an anthology film that was edited into an&nbsp;
incoherent mess? Try the fourth installment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.160
"Hellraiser: Bloodline." An unrelated script that&nbsp;
was grafted onto the series with little relation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:04.920
to what came before? Try the incredibly poorly&nbsp;
named "Hellraiser: Deader." The series didn't go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:10.260
direct-to-DVD and truly off the rails until the&nbsp;
fifth installment, 2000's "Hellraiser: Inferno,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:16.560
but sequels continued — and we also got a less&nbsp;
soulless and more thoughtful remake film in 2022.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.480
Released in 1990, Tremors is a classic creature&nbsp;
feature that you've probably stumbled upon while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:25.680
flipping channels across basic cable.&nbsp;
Starring Kevin Bacon and Fred Ward as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:29.820
couple of handymen in the down-and-out town&nbsp;
of Perfection, Nevada, it's an engaging and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:33.840
charming film featuring some very memorable&nbsp;
tunneling worm creatures with appetites for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.700
human flesh. With such impressive talent and&nbsp;
solid practical effects, it's no wonder that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.140
"Tremors" spawned a whole direct-to-video film&nbsp;
series — and even a short-lived television show.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:49.140
Though Fred Ward returned for 1996's not-bad&nbsp;
"Tremors 2: Aftershocks," succeeding entries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:53.700
saw diminishing returns. Whether it was the&nbsp;
introduction of flying raptor variations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:58.020
the series' monsters in "Tremors 3: Back&nbsp;
to Perfection," or a forgettable western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:03.660
prequel set in 1889, none of them ever matched&nbsp;
the explosive fun of the original. In 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:08.520
a fifth sequel was released co-starring Jamie&nbsp;
Kennedy — and that one isn't all that terrible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:13.440
Though the quality of the films may be uneven,&nbsp;
it's quite admirable that, throughout all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.300
installments and even the TV show, actor&nbsp;
Michael Gross always shows as the absurdly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:24.000
well-prepared survivalist Burt Gummer. The fun&nbsp;
that Gross has in the role is simply infectious.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.840
All you really need to know about the&nbsp;
original Leprechaun, released in 1993,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.700
is that a pre "Friends" Jennifer Aniston is in it&nbsp;
— a fact the marketing team got a lot of mileage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:37.800
out of on the film's eventual DVD release. These&nbsp;
movies have always been schlock amongst schlock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:43.140
puzzling at best — has anyone ever&nbsp;
found leprechauns to be scary? Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.380
the teams that have worked on the various&nbsp;
"Leprechaun" films at least deserve credit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:50.700
for hitting every single horror movie trope&nbsp;
setting over the years. The Leprechaun went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:55.020
to Vegas. The Leprechaun went to space.&nbsp;
The Leprechaun went to "the hood," — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.020 --> 00:03:00.060
it was actually pretty good. The Leprechaun&nbsp;
then went "back 2 tha hood," and it was not.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:04.380
A seventh installment, the first one&nbsp;
without original star Warwick Davis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:09.300
crawled out of the direct-to-video gutter for&nbsp;
a brief theatrical release and near-universal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:14.460
rejection in 2014. That still didn't stop&nbsp;
the leprechaun from returning in "Leprechaun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:19.500
Returns" four years later — but Davis didn't&nbsp;
appear in that one, either, and it aired on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:24.720
SyFy network. It seems this leprechaun hasn't&nbsp;
seen a big pot of gold in quite some time.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.200
"And now you're dead."

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:32.280
The original "Jarhead," released during the&nbsp;
height of the painful and questionable Iraq War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:37.320
was a gritty, realistic, personal movie about&nbsp;
the private hell of armed conflict. It wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:41.880
marked by big explosions and combat, but rather&nbsp;
the existential angst and terror that comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:46.020
during the moments in between. It was all set in&nbsp;
a surreal sandy landscape that would be beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:51.180
if it didn't invite so much dread. Directed by&nbsp;
Sam Mendes in collaboration with legendarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:57.000
talented cinematographer Roger Deakins, the&nbsp;
movie is gorgeous, stark, and unlike any war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.800
film you've seen this side of "Apocalypse&nbsp;
Now." It's simply great, plain and simple.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:07.440
And then there are the sequels, nine years&nbsp;
later. From the director of "Lake Placid:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.560
The Final Chapter" and "Company&nbsp;
of Heroes" came "Jarhead 2:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.820
Field of Fire." Then there's&nbsp;
"Jarhead 3: The Siege" and "Jarhead:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:20.400
Law of Return," which were so bad they're&nbsp;
barely worth talking about — so we won't.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:26.400
If the Brendan Fraser "Mummy" trilogy was like the&nbsp;
hit TV comedy ensemble "Friends," then the spinoff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.320
mythology series it gave birth to, The Scorpion&nbsp;
King, is like the "Joey" of Egyptian-flavored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:37.020
early-2000s action-horror. Introduced in 2001's&nbsp;
"The Mummy Returns," the Scorpion King was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:41.340
hulking silent heel played by Dwayne "The Rock"&nbsp;
Johnson in the early stages of his acting career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:45.900
back when people still weren't sure what he was&nbsp;
capable of. Though the movie wasn't reviewed well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:50.760
the Rock earned positive attention as a "Conan&nbsp;
with charisma" figure, and he has since built a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:54.780
career in Hollywood off of that initially&nbsp;
underestimated reputation. Surprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:58.860
the franchise he kicked off is still chugging&nbsp;
along as well, with a style that seems to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:03.720
more busy, more golden, and more like a "Mortal&nbsp;
Kombat" knockoff with every successive go.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:07.080
The 2017 remake of "The&nbsp;
Mummy" may have been a flop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.260
but could things be different for "The&nbsp;
Scorpion King?" We'll just have to wait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:13.800
and see — but if the answer is no,&nbsp;
we're not too broken up about it.

00:05:14.051 --> 00:05:21.251
"Nothing lasts forever, my king. And&nbsp;
that is the destiny of all kingdoms."

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.500
Fans of the science fiction film "Starship&nbsp;
Troopers" may be unaware of how old the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:31.740
franchise actually is. The source material for the&nbsp;
epic tale of man vs. giant alien bug was a 1959&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:37.320
novel written by Robert A. Heinlein, while the&nbsp;
first adaptation of the story was a 1988 six-part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:43.680
anime. Meanwhile, the 1997 film has built a legacy&nbsp;
of controversy, analysis, and debate thanks to its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:48.180
sharp and biting satire. Regardless of it all,&nbsp;
"Starship Troopers" has become an unstoppable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:52.260
franchise that extends to the present, with&nbsp;
a reboot rumored to still be in development.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:55.500
There is a surprising amount of&nbsp;
"Starship Troopers" content available,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.500 --> 00:06:01.740
including video games, an animated series, and&nbsp;
five feature-length films. The first two sequels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:06.000
"Hero of the Federation" and "Marauder,"&nbsp;
were direct-to-video projects. "Marauder"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.660
managed surprisingly decent reviews,&nbsp;
thanks to the return of Casper Van&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:14.220
Dien as beloved bug-killer Johnny Rico. Two&nbsp;
additional animated films, "Invasion" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:17.760
"Traitor of Mars," are must-watches&nbsp;
for only the most die-hard of fans.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:22.740
There's nothing in the rules that says a dog&nbsp;
can't play basketball — and there's no rule&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:27.360
that says that same dog can't spark a massive&nbsp;
franchise in the process, either. From its humble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:32.700
start in 1997 as a feel-good family movie about a&nbsp;
canine athlete and the boy who befriends him, "Air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:37.380
Bud's" evolution was slow at first. There was the&nbsp;
traditional sequel, released in theaters, in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:42.840
the dog plays football. In the new millennium, he&nbsp;
learned how to play soccer, baseball, and finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:48.000
volleyball, all of which made sense to varying&nbsp;
degrees. This could have easily gone on forever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.400
until someone had the brilliant idea to leave the&nbsp;
sports behind — as well as anything resembling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:58.920
reality, In 2006, "Air Buddies" introduced&nbsp;
talking dogs — and suddenly, all bets were off.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:03.660
Over the next seven years, the buddies&nbsp;
faced some serious snow, went to space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:08.580
did Halloween, made a lateral career move&nbsp;
into archaeology, gained superpowers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:14.100
and met Santa. "Santa Buddies" then split into its&nbsp;
own universe, with a prequel called "The Search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:19.140
for Santa Paws" and a sequel with a surprisingly&nbsp;
serious subplot about a widower and his children.

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:23.100
The world of "Air Bud" is honestly one&nbsp;
of the most confusing cinematic universes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:26.400
out there. The last "Air Buddies"&nbsp;
adventure was released back in 2013,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:29.340
but there's still time for the&nbsp;
buddies to broaden their horizons.

00:07:29.534 --> 00:07:36.434
"Broaden our horizons?! We're lost in space,&nbsp;
dog! I think our horizons are broad enough."

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:42.180
The original 1997 "Anaconda" film, about an&nbsp;
over-sized snake terrorizing a documentary crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:46.380
in the Amazon Rainforest, was a forgettable&nbsp;
project released amidst a barrage of killer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:51.900
creature movies including "Bats," "Deep Blue Sea,"&nbsp;
and "Lake Placid." Although it boasted an all-star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:57.960
'90s cast including Jennifer Lopez, Owen Wilson,&nbsp;
Jon Voight, and Ice Cube, the film has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:02.640
generally panned for its cookie-cutter characters&nbsp;
delivered with half-hearted performances. Despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:07.080
being nominated for six Razzie Awards, the&nbsp;
movie did well at the box office against a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:11.340
moderate budget. Of course, Hollywood could never&nbsp;
leave well enough alone with the snake movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:15.420
that exceeded expectations — and they didn't&nbsp;
hesitate to bleed the creature feature dry.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:21.180
"Anaconda" offers an unnecessary four sequels,&nbsp;
with the second film in the franchise, "Anacondas:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:26.100
The Hunt for the Blood Orchid," being the only one&nbsp;
released in theaters. "Anaconda 3 &amp; 4" were filmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:31.740
back-to-back for release on the SyFy Channel in&nbsp;
2008 and 2009. Finally, the fifth installment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:36.840
also a made-for-television production, involved a&nbsp;
crossover with another ongoing creature series in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:42.180
"Lake Placid vs. Anaconda." As misleading as the&nbsp;
title may be, the Amazonian snake does not fight a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:47.280
body of water, but rather the giant crocodiles&nbsp;
from the similar franchise. But "Anaconda"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:52.440
is still not done: as is the case with so many&nbsp;
other '90s franchises, a reboot is in the works.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:57.180
The original "Home Alone" is a cherished&nbsp;
Christmas film beloved by audiences of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:02.280
all ages. Everything about the first film fell&nbsp;
perfectly into place: it was written and directed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:07.260
by cinema legends John Hughes and Chris Columbus,&nbsp;
it featured a young charismatic Macauley Culkin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:11.580
and it even had an unforgettable score&nbsp;
from composer John Williams. Somehow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:15.360
these creators were even able to recreate&nbsp;
the magic with the sequel "Home Alone 2:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:20.460
Lost in New York." Unfortunately, "Home&nbsp;
Alone" was not a gift that kept on giving.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:27.062
"You got any more?!"

00:09:27.062 --> 00:09:27.900
Many fans of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:32.280
holiday classic may be unaware that the&nbsp;
"Home Alone" franchise now extends across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:38.400
six movies — some more forgettable than others.&nbsp;
1997's "Home Alone 3" was fortunate enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:42.600
feature a script from John Hughes, but it was&nbsp;
otherwise a broad departure from the originals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:47.580
Where the made-for-television "Home Alone 4"&nbsp;
went wrong was in its decision to recast original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:52.260
characters like Kevin McCallister and the Wet&nbsp;
Bandits without anybody from the original films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:56.760
taking part in the production. The fifth film,&nbsp;
"The Holiday Heist," was equally forgettable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:01.020
even if it did have Malcolm McDowell in it.&nbsp;
When Disney got their hands on the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:06.060
more effort was put into the direct-to-streaming&nbsp;
sequel "Home Sweet Home Alone." Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:10.080
the critically-panned sixth installment&nbsp;
ultimately destroyed the chances of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:14.267
potential Ryan Reynolds-led, cannabis-infused&nbsp;
"Home Alone" remake, "Stoned Alone."

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:19.800
Long before superhero films were dominating&nbsp;
theaters across the country, "The Crow" was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:24.480
one of the first successful projects based on a&nbsp;
comic book not named "Superman." Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:28.320
the movie is entwined with the tragedy&nbsp;
of star Brandon Lee's onset death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:32.820
as he was fatally wounded while filming.&nbsp;
However, the feature received positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:36.600
reviews and has since become a classic for&nbsp;
its dark visual tone and production design.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:40.440
The story of a resurrected musician&nbsp;
set on vengeance spawned an entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:45.120
franchise that includes three sequels and a&nbsp;
short-lived television series. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:47.820
each of the movies released&nbsp;
set the franchise further back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:51.120
culminating in the critically&nbsp;
derided "The Crow: Wicked Prayer."

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:55.440
Many fans still hope for "The Crow" to fly&nbsp;
to its former heights. Musician Rob Zombie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:00.540
once intended to make his directorial debut&nbsp;
with "The Crow: 2037," while rapper DMX also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:05.520
missed out on a potential "The Crow" project.&nbsp;
Thankfully, there is still hope for the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:10.200
as a remake of the 1994 film, starring&nbsp;
Bill Skarsgård, is soon to be released.

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:15.480
Ground-breaking for its era, the original&nbsp;
"The Land Before Time," released in 1988,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:20.280
was a children's movie with serious Hollywood&nbsp;
power behind it. Directed by animation legend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:26.160
Don Bluth, it also featured Steven Spielberg and&nbsp;
George Lucas as executive producers. A moderate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:30.180
success upon its release, the story about&nbsp;
a group of displaced young dinosaurs grew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:34.500
to gigantic proportions, releasing new&nbsp;
sequels far beyond what was necessary.

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:39.180
The first "Land Before Time" sequel actually&nbsp;
wasn't released until six years after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:44.220
original — and neither Bluth, Spielberg, nor&nbsp;
Lucas were attached to the project. Regardless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:49.560
the franchise began releasing a new movie almost&nbsp;
annually until 2007, along with a short-lived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:54.180
animated series that aired on Cartoon Network.&nbsp;
"Land Before Time" simply could not be stopped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:59.760
with "The Land Before Time XIV: Journey of the&nbsp;
Brave" releasing in 2016. Although rumored to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:03.360
be the final film in the dino-sized&nbsp;
collection, it's easy to imagine the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:07.935
"Land Before Time" franchise carrying on until&nbsp;
they run out of Roman numerals for the titles.

00:12:08.435 --> 00:12:12.240
The story of what happened at 112 Ocean&nbsp;
Avenue has become more than a legend:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:16.740
it's a paranormal dynasty that has outgrown&nbsp;
its humble beginnings. The original story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:22.320
as fictionalized in Jay Anson's 1977 novel "The&nbsp;
Amityville Horror," involves a young family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:27.120
moving into a colonial home in Amityville,&nbsp;
NY before quickly being forced to abandon it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:31.260
after some paranormal events. The original&nbsp;
book received its own literary sequels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:35.640
but nothing compares to the number of movies&nbsp;
spun off from the haunted residential home.

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:40.140
The original "Amityville Horror" movie&nbsp;
was released in 1979 — and since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:45.480
there has been a smorgasbord of remakes,&nbsp;
sequels, and satires. There are so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:49.980
"Amityville" features that it is nearly&nbsp;
impossible to keep track of them. Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:53.460
some are less faithful to the source&nbsp;
material than others — like "Amityville:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:57.360
Karen," about an entitled "Karen" who&nbsp;
becomes possessed by the paranormal house.

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:00.900
"Oh s—!"
"You don't have a permit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:04.826
to be jogging! These streets are made for cars!"

00:13:04.826 --> 00:13:10.500
Regardless, it is safe to say that&nbsp;
low-budget "Amityville" movies are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:13.980
and may forever be, a consistent&nbsp;
element of B-grade horror.

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:20.340
The 2003 slasher film "Wrong Turn" was a horror&nbsp;
movie nobody expected much from. The story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:24.780
three cannibal brothers lurking in the forests of&nbsp;
the Appalachian Mountains who capture and kill a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:29.820
group of young hikers, the movie did moderately&nbsp;
well both critically and financially. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:34.140
any success for a horror movie instantly&nbsp;
means a sequel is on the way. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:37.800
the franchise that was spawned from the&nbsp;
original film is nothing short of unexpected.

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:41.400
There are currently seven movies&nbsp;
based on the grisly story, the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:45.120
recent being the 2021 reboot originally&nbsp;
titled "Wrong Turn: The Foundation."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:49.920
This latest installment reimagines most of&nbsp;
the tale, with members of a centuries-old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:54.480
cult becoming the main antagonists. The other&nbsp;
five sequels featured the same horrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:59.501
brothers — Three Finger, Saw Tooth, and One Eye&nbsp;
— who terrorized Eliza Dushku in the original.

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:03.960
Who wouldn't love a story about a troublesome&nbsp;
yet lovable Saint Bernard who wins over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:08.280
hearts of its adoptive family while causing&nbsp;
chaos around their home? What if they made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:13.320
that into eight movies? And what if the dog&nbsp;
landed an acting job, uncovered lost treasure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:17.760
and was voiced by Tom Arnold? The "Beethoven"&nbsp;
franchise has certainly grown bigger than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:21.840
cumbersome pet since its humble beginnings&nbsp;
in the inaugural 1992 family comedy.

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:26.760
Originally written by celebrated filmmaker&nbsp;
John Hughes — under a pseudonym, as he didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:30.900
even want to be associated with the project —&nbsp;
"Beethoven" was a charming film that managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:35.700
rake in the money. So, of course, Hollywood&nbsp;
had to suck the beloved franchise bone dry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:40.440
It could be argued that "Beethoven's 2nd" was&nbsp;
a faithful and worthwhile sequel, but things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:44.880
definitely went downhill from there. The third&nbsp;
installment featured a cross-country road trip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:49.680
followed by a fourth movie involving a collection&nbsp;
of new pet friends — and by the seventh film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:53.534
Beethoven was being dubbed over by Tom&nbsp;
Arnold and helping to save Christmas.

00:14:53.534 --> 00:14:58.740
"You know, Beethoven, you must be really famous.&nbsp;
Cause it seems like every person that lays eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:01.946
on you is just left speechless."
"Yeah, I've done a few things."

00:15:01.946 --> 00:15:06.326
Much like the energetic Saint Bernard himself,&nbsp;
the "Beethoven" series is out of control.

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:11.700
This is one franchise that no one was asking&nbsp;
for — but it shot for the stars nonetheless.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:16.980
When the original "Sniper" was released in 1993,&nbsp;
critics praised its fun approach to a classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:22.200
style of psychological thriller. However, it was&nbsp;
consistently noted as being unoriginal or shallow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:27.060
For the most part, it seemed like "Sniper" had&nbsp;
been discharged — until almost 10 years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:31.560
when it came back for a sequel that formed&nbsp;
a B-grade franchise that simply won't stop.

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:36.300
There are now nine movies in the "Sniper"&nbsp;
franchise, with a tenth reportedly on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:41.160
way. Somehow, most of the low-budget films have&nbsp;
managed to maintain many of the stars from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:46.080
inaugural feature, including Billy Zane and&nbsp;
Tom Berenger. Still, each of the subsequent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:50.580
direct-to-video sequels continue to be buried deep&nbsp;
in the bargain bin, with the latest installment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:55.980
2022's "Sniper: Rogue Mission," flying heavily&nbsp;
under the radar of most media outlets. Whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.980 --> 00:16:00.780
anybody is watching or not, it seems as&nbsp;
if "Sniper" has many more stories to tell.

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:05.820
"Jaws" isn't the only shark movie that's been&nbsp;
run ashore with unnecessary sequels. Released 24&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:10.920
years after Steven Spielberg's classic, "Deep Blue&nbsp;
Sea" was another moderately successful '90s movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:15.935
that's perhaps best remembered for the scene where&nbsp;
Samuel L. Jackson is eaten by a gigantic shark.

00:16:16.134 --> 00:16:22.534
"We're gonna find a way to get out of here!&nbsp;
First, we're gonna seal off this roo—"

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:28.680
For the longest time, "Deep Blue Sea"&nbsp;
remained a standalone project embraced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:33.300
by action fans. However, Hollywood can&nbsp;
never resist a revival — and in 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:37.440
they began building a franchise out of&nbsp;
everyone's second-favorite shark movie.

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:42.900
"Deep Blue Sea 2" and "Deep Blue Sea 3" are both&nbsp;
direct-to-video films that carry on the story of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:47.880
the genetically modified sharks introduced in the&nbsp;
first movie. Surprisingly, the sequels are adored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:53.040
by fans and critics alike: in particular, the&nbsp;
R-rated "Deep Blue Sea 3" has earned itself a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:57.720
respectably "fresh" Rotten Tomatoes score — even&nbsp;
if they did miss the opportunity to call it "Deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:02.580
Blue Three." There's no report of a fourth film&nbsp;
in the franchise quite yet, but somebody out there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:06.801
just has to be planning a new installment&nbsp;
that's deeper and bluer than ever before.

